2. In Coding Quiz 1, there are two approaches to matching: 
(A) Picking the best match X = 0 corresponding to each X = 1 using Z values.
(B) Using radius_neighbors to pick all matches X = 0 within a distance of 0.2 of each X = 1.

Invent your own type of matching similar to 1 and 2 (or look one up on the internet), which has a different way to pick the matches in X = 0.  Clearly explain the approach you invented or found.

A type of matching that I found on the internet is called Coarsened Exact Matching (CEM). This is a matching system that relies on different variable values to match memebers of the control and treatment group. To begin this matching process, you first have to choose the group of variables you will use to match people. This is one of the hardest parts of this process. If variables that are too tight or too loose are choosen, then proper matching may not occur. Examples of these variables include age, gender, if someone has cancer, if someone has a different medical diagnosis, etc. 

Once you have your variables, you then will divide them into certain bins. Looking at age, this variable can be broken down in many different bins. For example, you could have it be binary where if someone is above a certain age, they have a 1, otherwise it is a 0. Another example of binning age would be to break down the age range into quartiles and everyone would have a number between 1 and 4. Determining how you breakdown different variables into bins relies on what your data looks like. For example, in a study where age is very diverse, using a binary bin system would probably be fine, but if ages were all above a certain number, that type of bin system would be too loose.

When all the variables have been turned into bins, each person will then be matched up to someone in the other cohort that has the same bin signature. A bin signature is someone's combination of bin numbers for each variable. Sometimes, someone in one cohort will not have any matches with someone in the other cohort. You also may notice that the number of people matched in the control group is not the same as the treatment. This variance must be treated with a weighting system, where unmatched people have a weight of 0, matched treatment people have a weight of one, and people that were matched in the control group are assigned a weight using the formula: (n_treatment/n_control)/(total_n_control/total_n_treatment). These weights can then be used to get the final weighted treatment effect without any variance by taking the evaluated measurements and multiplying them by the individual's weight.

