2. Write a Python program that performs a bootstrap simulation to find the variance in the mean of the Pareto distribution when different samples are taken.  Explain what you had to do for this.  As you make the full sample size bigger (for the same distribution), what happens to the variance of the mean of the samples?  Does it stay about the same, get smaller, or get bigger?

To code this, I started by creating a pareto distribution using np.random.pareto(a = 2, size = 10000) and created a list of sample sizes which were 10, 50, 100, 250, 500, 1000, 2500, 5000, and 10000. I then began using a for loop that would first loop through each sample size, and within the loop, create a list of sample means. I would then begin another loop that will run 1000 times that will randomly select the sample size amount of data points from the pareto distribution. I would then take the mean of this sample and append it to the sample means list. Once the loop that creates the random samples finishes, I take the variance of the sample means list and then display that variance. The loop will then start again with the next sample size until it has finished going through that list. The code is attached in the reflections code notebook I have submitted as well. 

As the full sample size becomes bigger, the variance of the mean begins to get smaller. For the smallest sample size of 10, the variance was 0.6568 and the largest sample size of 10,000 had a variance of 0.0007. I found that in the beginning, the smaller sample sizes have much larger decreases in the variance compared to the larger sample sizes. This does make sense since as the sample size gets larger, the samples are more likely to have similar data points, and therefore similar means which would lead to smaller variances. 

